/*--------------------------------------------
A Cassegrain reflecting telescope: a concave primary mirror and a convex secondary mirror.

Light from a distant object (a beam from infinity, 3 degrees off the axis so the three legs can be told apart) arrives from the left
and meets the primary mirror, a concave mirror with R = -200 mm (f = 100 mm). It is reflected back to the left, towards the primary's
own focus 100 mm in front of it - but the convex secondary mirror (R = -100 mm, f = -50 mm) stands 60 mm in front of the primary and
catches it first, sending it back to the right, through the (undrawn) hole in the primary, to a focus 140 mm behind the primary.

The system's equivalent focal length is f1 f2 / (f1 + f2 - d) = 100 x (-50) / (100 - 50 - 60) = 500 mm (2 D): a 100 mm mirror
behaving like a 500 mm telescope. I1 marks where the primary alone would have brought the light to a focus; the secondary cuts the light off before it gets
there, so I1 is a virtual object for the secondary and is drawn dashed, with dashed lines from the secondary to it.

Reflection is written with a NEGATIVE index after each mirror and NEGATIVE gaps while the light travels to the left; after the second
mirror the index and the gap are positive again.
----------------------------------------------*/
{
